  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 108 PM MST Tue Nov 3 2026\n\nFLOODING CONTINUES ALONG RIVERS, CREEKS, STREAMS:\n\nWhile today's forecast features dry conditions, flooding will \ncontinue along local rivers, creeks, streams, etc. as an \nabundance of water works through the system. Locations closer to\nthe headwaters will see declining river levels with many sites \nfalling below action stage today. Locations further downstream \nwill see delayed impacts with some just cresting and others yet \nto crest over the next 24 hours. Visit water.noaa.gov for the \nlatest river forecasts in your area. \n\nQUICK-HITTING MID WEEK SYSTEM: \n\nA low pressure system currently located in northern California \nwill track towards eastern Utah and western Colorado over the \nnext 36 hours. This progressive system will bring valley rain \nand mountain snow to the region Wednesday afternoon through \nWednesday night. Slick conditions are expected to develop over \nmountain passes with 3- 6\" of snow possible above 10,000 feet. \nLesser amounts are possible above 8,000 feet. Slow down and \nwatch for rapidly changing road conditions if you are out and \nabout!\n\nThe biggest point of concern and uncertainty with this system \nis how much convective enhancement will be realized on Wednesday\nafternoon. If rain/snow rates are enhanced from convection, the\npotential for impacts increases. Localized flash flooding is \npossible if stronger rain showers/storms develop over saturated \nor flood-prone areas, and hazardous travel conditions could \ndevelop over areas that see convective bursts of snow.\n\nLOOKING AHEAD: \n\nA dry forecast is favored for Thursday and beyond with warming \nabove seasonal normals by the weekend. The only thing that could\nderail the dry forecast is the passage of another fast-moving \nlow pressure system Friday PM. At this time, the more favorable \nmoisture looks to remain north of the local forecast area, but \nwe will continue to monitor this timeframe for some light valley\nrain/mountain snow.",
